# Every tracked text blob in this repository is already LF -- all 478 of them,
# checked before this file was added -- so these rules declare the existing
# state rather than changing it. Adding this renormalizes nothing.
#
# It exists because core.autocrlf is enabled on at least one workstation, which
# makes Git want CRLF in the working tree. That is harmless for a file Git only
# ever hands back to an editor. It is not harmless for a file a Linux runtime
# executes or parses, and the failure is quiet: a CRLF shebang makes bash exit
# with "$'\r': command not found" rather than anything that names the cause.
#
# The visible symptom of the missing rules was backend/lessons/unit_1/*.yaml
# showing as modified in git status with an empty diff -- Git wanting CRLF on
# disk, then normalizing straight back to the identical blob on commit.

* text=auto

# Run by bash in Vercel's Linux build container. CRLF here breaks the Ignored
# Build Step, which fails safe by building, so a broken one looks like a step
# that simply never skips.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Parsed by the Python backend on Render and by the Node lesson tooling.
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Shared across the web app, the Expo app, and CI.
*.js   text eol=lf
*.cjs  text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.tsx  text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf

# Course media. Marked explicitly so Git never attempts a text conversion on
# them, rather than trusting content detection across ~3400 files.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.svg   text eol=lf
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary
*.mp3   binary
*.wav   binary
*.m4a   binary
*.pptx  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary

# Signing material, should any ever be committed by accident.
*.jks      binary
*.keystore binary
*.p12      binary
*.p8       binary
